  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "@guowenzhang/dsh-ui-beautify",
      "text": "English | [中文](<README.zh.md>)"
    },
    {
      "section": "Background",
      "text": "A third-party interface enhancement plugin for [DeepSeek Harness](https://deepseek-harness.github.io/deepseek-harness/), focused on **mobile usability**: making conversations, the sidebar, and settings easier to use on small screens."
    },
    {
      "section": "Background",
      "text": "It also offers independent body and code font choices, custom logos, icons, names and welcome taglines, and translation of English skill and plugin descriptions into the interface language using the default model. Other improvements include recent conversation tabs, upward navigation through your sent questions (which loads the older page once the loaded history runs out), a floating one-line preview of the round you are reading that jumps back to its start, a composer light beam, and desktop quick replies."
    },
    {
      "section": "Background",
      "text": "Every choice on the settings page reports its own write: the row says it is saving, confirms when the value landed, and offers a retry when the Host refuses it. The name and tagline fields wait for an input method to finish before they commit, so confirming a candidate never saves half a word.\n\nOn phones whose browser predates the newest JavaScript APIs, the plugin completes the ones the PDF engine calls, so PDF and Word/PowerPoint previews open instead of failing with `... is not a function`."
    },
    {
      "section": "Installation",
      "text": "Requires DeepSeek Harness **0.2.0-rc.1 or newer**. This example uses the `web` profile:\n\n```sh\nnpx @deepseek-ai/dsh plugin --profile web add @guowenzhang/dsh-ui-beautify\n```\n\nRestart the corresponding host and reload the browser, then open **Settings → UI Beautify**. Click **Translate descriptions** to generate translations; this uses the default model's quota, caches results, and leaves original files unchanged."
    },
    {
      "section": "Installation",
      "text": "Translations sharing the same local data directory are deduplicated across host processes, including recovery after an owner exits. Unknown locks are preserved rather than deleted. Recovery retains small generation-marker directories to prevent stale processes from taking a newer lock; do not clean these markers while any host is running."
    },
    {
      "section": "Version and upgrade",
      "text": "General settings gains one line under **Current version**: the newest release published to the desktop shell's own update feed, with an **Upgrade** button beside it. The button opens the shell's existing update flow, so downloading and installing stay with the updater that owns them; when the running install is already the newest release, the line says so and the button is disabled."
    },
    {
      "section": "Version and upgrade",
      "text": "The line appears only where a feed exists. A desktop build carries `resources/app-update.yml` naming its feed URL and channel, which is what the row reads; a plain `dsh web` install has no such file and sees no row. A deployment that publishes elsewhere — an internal mirror, a different channel — names its manifest explicitly:\n\n```yaml\n- id: ui-beautify\n  name: '@guowenzhang/dsh-ui-beautify'\n  config:\n    updateFeedUrl: 'https://example.com/releases/latest.yml'"
    },
    {
      "section": "Recent conversations",
      "text": "Recent conversations have their own switch in **Settings → UI Beautify** and do not require the code work view or mobile adaptation. The strip shows up to five eligible conversations in activity order; selecting one does not reorder it. Tabs use the host theme tokens in light and dark mode, including when the host has no conversation/trace tab bar."
    },
    {
      "section": "Recent conversations",
      "text": "On desktop the strip is a hemp line pinned to the header's left and right edges. Up to five recent conversations hang from it on clothespins in the middle, the selected conversation is tinted, and the line sags toward the middle. The line shares the row with the host conversation/trace tabs, which stay clickable; the header grows by the line height. When the header narrows, the tags shrink and then move to their own row below the host tabs, and the line still reaches both edges. At widths of 600px or less, phones keep the previous text-tab strip and hide the host view tabs. A host-native recent strip takes precedence. Disabling recent conversations or unloading the plugin removes the strip and restores the host tabs."
    },
    {
      "section": "Recent conversations",
      "text": "For development, `pnpm run test:recent` runs catalog/status tests and a real Chrome/React fixture at 1200, 601, 600 and 393px, including delayed tab bars, the band/stack fallbacks, session navigation and cleanup. It uses the existing desktop-profile Playwright installation and local Chrome; set `DSH_MOBILE_PLAYWRIGHT` and `DSH_MOBILE_CHROME` to use other installations. It does not launch or change a running DSH host."
    },
    {
      "section": "Unread conversations",
      "text": "When a run finishes while you are viewing another conversation, the sidebar marks that conversation with a green dot. Opening it lands at the beginning of its latest answer instead of the bottom of the transcript, so the answer can be read from its first line. The landing waits for the host to restore the conversation, uses the same reading inset as the navigation controls, and backs off as soon as you scroll yourself. Its switch, **Unread conversation opens at the answer**, lives in **Settings → UI Beautify → Interface enhancements** and defaults on."
    },
    {
      "section": "Floating quick actions",
      "text": "A draggable button floats at the bottom-right of an open conversation. Its position is kept in the browser, so it survives a reload, and the button rests just above the message box rather than over its send button; drag it anywhere inside the window. Clicking it opens a host menu with three shortcuts for the conversation on screen:"
    },
    {
      "section": "Floating quick actions",
